Who’s really funding election campaigns video :-
- Campaign funding laws go into 3 categories
    1. Party spending
    2. Candidate spending
    3. 3rd party spending - businesses, charities, trade unions
- Candidate spending
    - Begins when parliament dissolve and 5 weeks after that during election season
    - £8700 in their constituency + £0.06 per voter in borough constituencies or £0.09 per voter in rural constituencies
    - Usually £10000-£16000 → Corbyn in 2019 was £13,209.72

- Political parties elections and referendums act 2000
    - Established the Electoral commission who publishes candidate spending at
    - [Campaign spending: Candidates](https://www.electoralcommission.org.uk/who-we-are-and-what-we-do/financial-reporting/campaign-spending-candidates)
    - All donations over £50 must be reported to the EC and must have a permissable source
        - Permissable source - any voter on the electoral register or a company registered in the U.K.
- Third party spending
    - The Lobbying act 2014
    - Third party campaign spending has to be backdated a year
    - Any third party with over £20,000 spent in England or £10,000 or Scotland, Wales and NI must register with the EC
    - Spending limit on third parties -
        - U.K. - £584,817
        - Constituency - £9750
- Party spending
    - Backdated for longer usually a year
    - £30,000 per constituency per party
    - £810,000 per England
    - Whichever one is higher
    - £30,000 x 650 = 19.5 million
    - Anything £500 must come from a permissable source
    - Anything above £1500 must be immediately reported to the EC
- Problems:
    - Blurred between candidate spending and party spending - hard to track
    - Money is only spent in the marginal seats that votes aren’t already spent
        - Want to pass this off as national spending so they can have more to spend
        - In 2017 Conservatives were caught guilty of this when using Battle busses in the 2015 election to move volunteers into Thanet and declaring it as national spending
        - Fined £70,000 by the EC
    - Online campaigning has blurred the lines even more
        - Targeted online campaigning is almost impossible to miss
        - Online advertising is more effective and gives wealthier parties an even bigger advantage than before (e.g. Brexit leave campaign)

Additional funding -
- Policy development grants:
    - Allocate £2 million to all the main parties so that they can employ policy advisers
- Short Money:
    - Named after the Labour politician Ted Short, is allocated to the opposition parties for their work in the House of Commons based on the number of seats they have. The leader of the opposition is also funded almost £800,000 for the running of their office
    - Short Money 2021-22 - Labour £6,602,347, SNP £1,117,489, Lib Dems £903,753, DUP £195,870
- Cranborne Money:
    - Named after the Conservative peer Lord Cranborne, subsidies the work of scrutiny carried out by the opposition parties in the house of lords
- Cash for Honours:
    - Tony Blair’s final years as prime minister (2006-2007) was blighted by the ‘cash for honours’ scandal when it was suggested that certain Labour donors had been elevated to the House of Lords because of donations to the Labour Party. Although there were no prosecutions, there was so much outrage that Blair commissioned the Phillips report to investigate the case for party funding reform. The Phillips Report (2007) concluded that there was a strong case for political parties to be primarily funded through taxation and for a limit of £50,000 to be put on donations from individuals and organisations
    - However, these recommendations have not been acted upon. The Conservative Party, as the largest recipient of donations, is unwilling to lose that advantage over its rivals. Labour is also wary of reforms that would remove the financial support receives from trade unions.
